# Line endings are load-bearing here, not cosmetic.
#
# This package is byte-compared against another package's copy of the same file: the suite in
# ../pick_ik_mcp asserts that `mcp_protocol.py` here and `pick_ik_mcp/vendored/mcp_protocol.py`
# there are the same bytes. This machine runs with core.autocrlf=true (git says so unprompted:
# "in the working copy of '__init__.py', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches
# it"), and the sibling repo already pins its side to LF. Without the same pin here, a fresh clone
# would materialise these files with CRLF and that check would fail on line endings alone, with
# nothing wrong in a single byte of logic.
# Line 10 pins every tracked file git treats as text to LF so the cross-package byte-compare of
# mcp_protocol.py (line 3-9) survives a fresh clone on this core.autocrlf=true box. text=AUTO so git's
# own binary detection is what decides; the per-extension lines below carry the real intent either way,
# and the explicit `binary` set is what keeps bytes-exact things (the STLs above all) out of the filter.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf

# Never run the line-ending machinery over these: they are compared, hashed or loaded as bytes.
# The *.stl line is load-bearing for the SPM FK-MVP: meshes/spm/SPM_STL.sha256.txt commits "the meshes,
# as committed", and under the old `* text eol=lf` the clean filter collapsed CR-LF pairs out of 3 of the
# 8 committed STL blobs (bending the geometry Blender loads and breaking that manifest). Mark them binary
# so the byte-exact committed blob equals the working copy by construction, for every STL in the repo.
*.dll    binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary
*.pdb    binary
*.pyc    binary
*.stl    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.zip    binary
*.rar    binary
*.blend  binary
*.glb    binary
